{{smaller|'''Alleghany vine''', climbing fumitory, ''Adlumia cirrhosa.''—'''Harvey’s vine.''' See ''Sarcopetalum.''—'''India-rubber vine.''' See ''india-rubber.''—'''Isle-of-Wight vine.''' See ''Tamus.''—'''Mexican vine.''' Same as ''Madeira-vine.''—'''Milk vine.''' (''a'') See ''Periploca.'' (''b'') A plant of Jamaica, ''Forsteronia floribunda'' of the ''Apocynaceæ'', yielding an excellent caoutchouc.—'''Red-bead vine''', ''Abrus precatorius.'' See ''Abrus.''—'''Scrub vine''', an Australian plant of the genus ''Cassytha'', especially ''C. melantha.'' The species are leafless parasites with filiform or wiry twining stems resembling dodder. Though anomalous in habit, the genus is classed in the ''Laurineæ'' on account of the structure of the flowers.—'''Seven-year vine''', a plant of the morning-glory kind, ''Ipomæa tuberosa'', widely diffused through the tropics. It has a very large tuber, and climbs to the top of high trees; the flowers are 2 inches long, bright-yellow. Also ''Spanish arbor-vine.''—'''Sorrel vine.''' See ''sorrel-vine.''—'''Spanish arbor-vine.''' Same as ''seven-year vine.''—'''To dwell under one’s vine and fig-tree.''' See ''dwell.''—'''Vine bark-louse.''' (''a'') ''Pulvinaria vitis'', a large coccid with large white egg-sac, common on the vine in Europe. (''b'') ''Aspidiotus uvæ'', a small, round, inconspicuous scale occurring on grape-canes in the United States; also, ''A. vitis'', a closely allied species occurring in Europe.—'''Vine cidaria.''' Same as ''vine inch-worm.''—'''Vine colaspis''', a leaf-beetle, ''Colaspis brun-''}}

